.\" Everything in mc.1 above the command list. The .TH line and the COMMANDS
.\" section are generated from the clap tree by `xtask man`; this file is the
.\" prose a parser cannot know. Raw roff: it is emitted verbatim, so escape
.\" literal hyphens as \- yourself.
.SH NAME
mc \- Minecraft server lifecycle manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mc
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mc
manages a single Minecraft server instance on bare metal, or inside a VM, LXC
container or Docker image. It installs and upgrades Vanilla, Paper, Fabric and
NeoForge servers, drives the systemd lifecycle, and owns the server's
configuration. Java is selected from the Minecraft version being installed, and
installed for you if it is missing.
.PP
There is one server per host. mc does not manage instances, because the thing
it manages \(em a service account, a unit, a data directory, a port \(em is
already singular on the machine.
.SS Plugins
Core installs, upgrades and runs the server. Everything else arrives as another
.BR .deb :
a plugin drops a manifest into
.I /usr/lib/mc/plugins.d/
and an executable into
.IR /usr/libexec/mc/ ,
and contributes subcommands, lifecycle hooks, or support for a new kind of
server artifact.
.B mc plugins
lists what is installed and what each one adds; a command that needs a plugin
you do not have names the package that provides it. See
.BR mc-plugins (5)
for the manifest format.
.SS Configuration
Two files, split by what they describe.
.I /etc/minecraft/config.toml
is
.IR mc 's:
which build to install, which Java, how much heap, how often to back up. See
.BR mc-config (5).
.PP
.I /opt/minecraft/server.properties
is the
.IR server 's:
port, seed, MOTD, difficulty, RCON. The JVM reads and rewrites that file, so mc
keeps no copy of anything in it \(em edit it directly. mc re-applies only the
keys it owns: the game port
.RB ( server-port ),
and both consoles' credentials
.RB ( enable-rcon ,
.BR rcon.port ,
.BR rcon.password ,
.BR management-server-enabled ,
.BR management-server-host ,
.BR management-server-port ,
.BR management-server-secret ).
They are re-applied after a modpack merges its own settings, so a pack cannot
choose either console's credential, or switch one on with a secret of its own.
.SS Privilege
Commands that write the server directory, the config or the unit state need
root. When you forget
.BR sudo (8)
and there is a terminal to prompt on, mc re-runs itself under it.
.PP
Read-only commands
.RB ( status ,
.BR logs ,
.BR plugins ,
.BR "rcon status" )
need no root at all if you are in the
.B minecraft
group:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo usermod \-aG minecraft "$USER"
.fi
.RE
